{
  "seq": 15,
  "kind": "turn",
  "at": "2026-10-02T11:09:14.603Z",
  "chatId": "2026-10-02@07h56m54s743ms",
  "line": "A month is an insult. Show me the original pawn ticket, or tell me which district's ledger I should start with.",
  "ok": true,
  "problems": [],
  "replied": true,
  "replies": [
    {
      "messageId": 6,
      "speaker": "The Pawnbroker",
      "text": "The Queen's Agent: The agent's jaw tightens, a flicker of frustration breaking through the polished mask. She looks at the list on the counter, then at the Pawnbroker.\n\"The original ticket is in the Keeper of Debts' private vault. If you want to see it, you will have to make an appointment with the woman who spends her nights editing the crown's history. I suggest you don't.\" Her voice drops, barely above a whisper. \"The Keeper does not like being reminded of things she has tried to forget.\"\nShe taps the list with one finger. \"The daughter of Merchant-Prince Voss lives in the Jade District, where the wealthy hoard memories like pearls. The memory-broker, the woman who handled the original transaction, operates from the Low Markets in the Silk District. And the third fragment, the largest one, is still being held in the crown's own Debt Hall. The Keeper is keeping it under lock and key, listed under the false name.\"\nThe agent's eyes search the Pawnbroker's face. \"A month's share is the price for the risk. You will have to infiltrate the Jade District's social circles, negotiate with a broker who has seen every kind of desperation, and—if you are truly foolish—attempt to extract information from the crown's most dangerous servant.\"\nShe folds her arms. \"If you want a higher price, you'll have to explain why you're worth it. Tell me what you have that can buy seven years of a queen's life back from the shadows.\""
    },
    {
      "messageId": 7,
      "speaker": "The Queen's Agent",
      "text": "*The agent's face remains impassive, though there is a cold stillness to her posture that suggests she has considered the word 'insult' and found it wanting.*\n\"The original pawn ticket is not mine to give,\" *she says, her voice measured.* \"It rests in the Keeper of Debts' private vault, where it has remained for years. The palace does not part with such things lightly—nor with its secrets.\"\n*She leans forward slightly, her tone shifting from command to something more like a confidence shared in the shadow of the counter.* \"If you seek a starting point, begin with the Silk District. Mira Vane, the broker there, has handled more pawned childhoods than most citizens have had years of their own. She will not give you names freely, but she will tell you which houses are trading in royal fragments and what currencies they accept.\"\n*The agent pauses, her gaze unwavering.* \"And as for the price—you mistake the offer for a wage. It is not. A month's share of a queen's childhood is a commodity of immense value in the memory market. But the true payment for this commission is something else entirely.\"\n*She straightens, returning to her formal bearing.* \"When the years are recovered, your name will be stricken from every ledger-house in this city. Your debts—public, private, and secret—will be erased. You will walk the streets as no one else does: with a clean slate.\""
    }
  ],
